Run host regressions from the repository root:

    python3 test/run_native.py

Requires g++ (or CXX), gcc (or CC), and the Arduino-Pico framework installed
by `pio run`.
Set ARDUINO_PICO_DIR when the framework is installed outside the default
~/.platformio/packages/framework-arduinopico directory. Tests use the actual
BTstack declarations and event accessors with fake hardware, filesystem, and
radio implementations. AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer errors
fail the run. CI runs these tests after building both firmware targets.

Coverage includes:
- input-voltage ADC setup, pin selection, divider scaling, millivolt encoding,
  and full status packets with voltage, timing, and identify acknowledgements;
- identify LED timing, restoration of DIO state, repeated requests, and clock
  rollover; identify packet validation, sequencing, watchdog isolation, and
  command acknowledgements alongside sensor telemetry;
- bounded diagnostic logging with a disconnected or stalled USB host, a busy
  USB mutex, partial writes, ring wraparound, full queues, reentrant callers,
  message truncation, and per-category rate limiting across clock rollover;
- motor-command processing and watchdog shutdown with a full diagnostic queue
  and stalled USB reader, and USB draining after control processing;
- packet lengths, all fixed-field masks, duplicate/stale sequences, rollover,
  arbitrary initial sequences, watchdog resets, and rename ACK/timing echoes;
- degree-to-servo round trips and analog/encoder boundary values;
- INI parsing, integer overflow, escaped names, and config preservation on
  short writes, failed close/sync, failed rename, and failed open;
- L2CAP retained SDU buffers, coalescing, inline/deferred callbacks, request
  errors, pending channel reservations, queue overflow, and foreign peers;
- GATT fallback with a credit-starved L2CAP channel still open, switching back
  to L2CAP, and delayed callbacks from the previously selected transport;
- the actual main loop during fast L2CAP reconnects with reused handles/CIDs,
  either LE/ATT event order, queued RX/TX, sequence zero before watchdog expiry,
  L2CAP-only closure, and disconnect without a new packet;
- identification-file creation and content updates, skipping writes for matching
  files, name refresh, and runtime diagnostics without filesystem writes;
- output shutdown before rename writes, queued enables during the reboot
  delay, repeated ACK windows, and status encoding of encoder wrap/sentinels;
- the actual bundled BTstack allocator with the project patch: 1,024 LE channel
  allocations, wraparound at 0x007f, occupied channels, and Classic/LE interleaving.

Hardware retest (native tests cannot reproduce controller/OS radio timing):
1. On Linux/macOS L2CAP, disconnect/reconnect at least 128 times without
   rebooting XRP, crossing the 64-channel LE CID range twice. Include restarting
   the client and reconnecting within 500 ms. Confirm
   controls and status resume, and queued commands do not survive disconnect.
2. Run through sequence 65535 -> 0 (about 22 minutes at 50 packets/second).
3. Repeat with a busy link and with a second client attempting to connect.
4. Rename while enabled: outputs should stop, a success ACK should arrive,
   and reconnect should work under the new name after reboot.
5. Smoke-test GATT, including notification disable/re-enable and reconnect.

The protocol uses modular sequence comparisons and automatically enables GATT
notifications when control traffic arrives.

Serial latency retest:
1. Capture USB Serial during normal control traffic and verify the periodic
   summaries and connection events arrive without per-packet logging.
2. Leave the serial port open while pausing host reads long enough for USB
   buffers to fill. Confirm motor commands remain responsive and the watchdog
   disables outputs when control traffic stops.
3. Resume reading and check log_drop/log_supp counters. Repeat with USB
   unplugged and during repeated Bluetooth connection failures.

Identify retest:
1. Connect with matching client and firmware builds and press Identify while
   disabled. Check for five seconds of LED flashing without enabling outputs.
2. Change DIO 1 during the flash and confirm that value is restored afterward.
3. Press Identify again during a flash to restart it. Disconnect during a flash
   and confirm it still finishes locally. Check sensor updates during the flash.

Input voltage retest (beta and production boards):
1. Flash the firmware and run the matching client. Compare Input voltage in
   XRP Status with the supply measured at board VIN and ground. Check the
   robot program's RobotController.getBatteryVoltage() / GetBatteryVoltage()
   agrees with the GUI reading.
2. Test battery and USB power, and confirm the reading changes with the
   measured supply. VIN is after the power switch; USB can supply VIN when
   the switch is on.
3. Repeat over L2CAP and GATT, after reconnecting, and during Identify. Check
   that voltage, other sensors, and latency continue updating.
